2963}],"values":{},"circuit_facts":[{"label":"kim_theta_0.0pi","cz_count":255,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]},{"label":"kim_theta_0.2pi","cz_count":255,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]},{"label":"kim_theta_0.35pi","cz_count":255,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]},{"label":"kim_theta_0.5pi","cz_count":255,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]}],"answer":"I ran one job (01a16485-2824-7104-ab34-2dd6a02f6c24) on emerald:mock, IQM's free test version of the 54-qubit Emerald chip. It completed and cost 0 credits. The job held 4 circuits with 250 shots each.\n\nWhat I built: every one of the 54 qubits starts at 0. One time step tilts every spin by the field angle theta. Then it couples each pair of neighbors once. The real Emerald currently lists 85 couplers, so each step has 85 two-qubit gates. Three steps give 255. I wrote each coupling gate as one CZ plus single-qubit turns that I folded into the next rotation. I sent these native gates straight to the server, so no compiler could add, merge, or remove a CZ. I read the job back from the server. Each circuit has exactly 255 CZ gates, which is 85 different couplers used 3 times. All 54 qubits (QB1 to QB54) are measured. The test device lists 90 couplers. All 85 real ones are among them, and I did not use the 5 extras.\n\nWhat a perfect quantum computer would show. I simulated the exact circuits I sent, with no noise. Magnetization is the average spin, where +1 means every spin points up.\n- theta = 0: 1.000. With no field, nothing tilts, so the sheet stays fully magnetized.\n- theta = 0.2 pi: 0.452. A weak field already removes more than half the magnetization in 3 steps.\n- theta = 0.35 pi: 0.113. A stronger field leaves almost nothing.\n- theta = 0.5 pi: 0.000. A field this strong flips each spin a quarter turn each step, and the average up-or-down value is exactly zero.\nThe principle: the sideways field pushes each spin away from up. The neighbor couplings then spread and scramble that change across the sheet. A stronger field melts the order faster. I checked the simulation two ways. A full simulation of a 14-qubit piece agreed with my shortcut method to about 1e-15. My native gates also gave the same result as the textbook coupling gate.\n\nWhat the test device returned: 0.013, -0.003, 0.019, and 0.011 for the four fields. All four are near zero, even at theta = 0, where the answer must be 1. That is expected. The mock device checks that a job is valid and runs it, but its counts do not depend on the circuit. So these numbers say nothing about physics. Only a run on the real Emerald would show the melting curve. That run would cost credits, and noise would pull all four values toward zero.\n\nThe four counts tables are in counts_list, one per circuit. I left the single counts field empty because the job has 4 circuits. Scratch files and results are in /Users/claytonchancey/Desktop/quantum/.claude/worktrees/wf_8d7e6233-d69-13/ (run_job.py, kim.py, results.json, job_log.txt)."}
